---
/**
 * `/data-terms` — the URL every published Optfall payload carries.
 *
 * `docs/DATA-TERMS.md` has each dataset embed
 * `"terms": "https://optfall.com/data-terms"`, and until this page existed that
 * link 404'd. A terms URL that does not resolve is worse than none: it is a
 * claim of provenance a recipient cannot check, on a project whose whole thesis
 * is being right.
 *
 * The page is deliberately a SUMMARY that points at the canonical document
 * rather than a second copy of it. Two prose copies of a licensing position
 * drift, and the one people would find first is the one nobody edits. The
 * disclaimer is the exception — it is required verbatim in the footer of every
 * page, which `scripts/check-disclaimer.ts` enforces against the built output.
 */
import BaseLayout from "../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";

const CANONICAL =
  "https://github.com/alxjrvs/optfall/blob/main/docs/DATA-TERMS.md";
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Data terms — Optfall"
  description="Terms on the data Optfall publishes: what is openly licensed, and what belongs to Legend Story Studios."
>
  <h1>Data terms</h1>

  <p>
    This page summarises the terms on data Optfall publishes. The canonical text is <a href={CANONICAL}><code>docs/DATA-TERMS.md</code></a>, and where the two differ, that document governs.
  </p>

  <h2>What is openly licensed</h2>

  <p>
    The <strong>structural work this project owns</strong>: rule identifiers, the legality timeline, version diffs, and annotations. These are ours to license, and they are published openly so that if this site disappears the corpus does not.
  </p>

  <h2>What is not ours to license</h2>

  <p>
    <strong>Card names, card text and card art remain the property of Legend Story Studios</strong> and are never relicensed here. Optfall uses them under LSS's published policy for third-party applications, which permits card databases and rules-enforcement tools on conditions that bind anyone we pass the data to as well.
  </p>

  <p>
    So a dataset from Optfall carries two things at once: an open licence over the structure, and LSS's conditions over the game content inside it. Redistributing one without the other is not something this project can authorise.
  </p>

  <h2>The copyright line, and how it is guaranteed</h2>

  <!--
    THIS SECTION HAS NOW BEEN WRONG IN BOTH DIRECTIONS, which is worth recording
    because the failure mode is identical each time: a compliance page whose
    claim is prose rather than a description of the code.

    First it described a component that did not exist — it said the notice "is
    rendered by the component itself", which was the CONTRACT
    (`CARD_IMAGE_COPYRIGHT` and `CardImageProps`) and not an implementation.
    That was corrected to "Optfall shows no card faces today", which was true
    when written.

    It stopped being true when `CardFace.svelte` and `CardFaceGroup.svelte`
    landed and the card pages began rendering artwork. So this page went on
    telling anyone checking our compliance position that we show no card images,
    on a site that shows a hero face and a printings rail on every card page —
    up to 22 images on one of them — eight on the front door, and a thumbnail
    per result in the search grid. A page whose whole job is to be accurate
    about exactly this.

    The lesson both times: state what the code does and name the file, so the
    claim is checkable rather than remembered.

    Inline elements stay on the same source line as the text around them: the
    build compresses this HTML and drops the whitespace at a line break falling
    immediately before an inline tag.
  -->
  <p>
    Wherever card images are shown, the notice <code>© Legend Story Studios</code> must accompany them. <strong>Optfall shows card faces, and the notice is emitted by the component that renders them rather than by the page.</strong> <code>CardFace</code> writes the line itself on every render — there is deliberately no <code>copyright</code> prop, so a caller cannot omit it, empty it or reword it. Where several faces are shown together on one screen, <code>CardFaceGroup</code> carries one notice for the group and suppresses the per-face line, so one line accompanies the images rather than repeating beneath each of them.
  </p>

  <p>
    That is the difference between a contract and a convention: a requirement a caller can satisfy incorrectly is a convention. <strong>No arrangement of these components renders a card image with no notice at all</strong> — that part is structural, and it is the claim this page is making. Two things are <em>not</em> mechanically checked, and both are judgements a page can get wrong.
  </p>

  <p>
    The first is whether a group is a reasonable one. Grouping asserts that the faces are seen together, and a caller who grouped faces a reader would have to scroll between would produce a notice that accompanies fewer images than it covers. The rule and its reasoning are written where the component is defined — and one grouping on this site, the long printings rail on a card page, currently sits on the wrong side of it. It is recorded as open rather than described as fine.
  </p>

  <p>
    The second is that a page's own layout can hide a notice the component correctly emitted — and this has happened here. The card fan on the front door was first built with its crop on the outer element, which cut the hoisted line in half; it was caught by looking at the page. Nothing in the markup was wrong, which is the point: the guarantee is that the notice is <em>rendered</em>, and a stylesheet can still put it somewhere nobody reads it.
  </p>

  <h2>The card data</h2>

  <p>
    The card corpus Optfall publishes is a trimmed, commit-pinned copy of an actively maintained community dataset, and it carries its own rights notice inside the file. Only Optfall's structural work over it — the field selection, the upstream pin, the derived legality verdicts and this envelope — is openly licensed. The card names and card text inside it are Legend Story Studios' and are not relicensed by us or by anyone we pass the file to.
  </p>
</BaseLayout>
